"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,625\nThe Learning Brain. Series 4: Habits\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,331\nEpisode 10\n\n3\n00:00:07,411 --> 00:00:09,686\nIntrinsic motivation\n\n4\n00:00:12,486 --> 00:00:15,125\nThink of something you study because you want to,\n\n5\n00:00:15,308 --> 00:00:18,359\nand something you study only because it is required.\n\n6\n00:00:18,787 --> 00:00:21,573\nThey feel different, and that difference matters.\n\n7\n00:00:21,743 --> 00:00:29,008\nPsychologists call the first intrinsic motivation: doing something because it is interesting or meaningful to you.\n\n8\n00:00:29,586 --> 00:00:33,687\nSelf determination theory says it grows when three needs are met: autonomy,\n\n9\n00:00:34,137 --> 00:00:38,032\ncompetence, and feeling connected to others.\n\n10\n00:00:38,202 --> 00:00:39,872\nRewards can backfire.\n\n11\n00:00:40,309 --> 00:00:45,029\nA meta-analysis of a hundred and twenty eight experiments found that expected,\n\n12\n00:00:45,208 --> 00:00:51,146\ntangible rewards for an interesting task reduced people's later free choice to keep doing it.\n\n13\n00:00:51,775 --> 00:00:53,940\nPositive feedback did the opposite,\n\n14\n00:00:54,149 --> 00:00:55,651\nand raised interest.\n\n15\n00:00:55,821 --> 00:00:56,785\nChoice helps.\n\n16\n00:00:57,122 --> 00:00:58,715\nAcross forty one studies,\n\n17\n00:00:58,735 --> 00:01:01,781\nhaving choices increased intrinsic motivation,\n\n18\n00:01:01,908 --> 00:01:03,406\neffort, and performance.\n\n19\n00:01:03,814 --> 00:01:07,083\nEffects were strongest with a few successive choices,\n\n20\n00:01:07,309 --> 00:01:08,464\naround two to four,\n\n21\n00:01:08,484 --> 00:01:09,614\nrather than one.\n\n22\n00:01:09,784 --> 00:01:11,110\nRelevance helps too.\n\n23\n00:01:11,618 --> 00:01:13,622\nIn high school science classes,\n\n24\n00:01:13,795 --> 00:01:18,996\nstudents who wrote about how the material connected to their own lives became more interested.\n\n25\n00:01:19,523 --> 00:01:24,253\nThose with low expectations earned about two thirds of a letter grade more.\n\n26\n00:01:24,781 --> 00:01:29,233\nA later study found a similar benefit for lower performing students.\n\n27\n00:01:29,403 --> 00:01:30,388\nSome caution.\n\n28\n00:01:30,774 --> 00:01:33,912\nMany reward studies used simple lab puzzles.\n\n29\n00:01:34,232 --> 00:01:37,136\nRewards can still help you start a dull task.\n\n30\n00:01:37,397 --> 00:01:39,609\nAnd you will not love every subject.\n\n31\n00:01:39,912 --> 00:01:42,692\nThe aim is to find some reason of your own.\n\n32\n00:01:42,862 --> 00:01:43,734\nTry this.\n\n33\n00:01:44,086 --> 00:01:48,669\nFor each course, write a few sentences on how it connects to your life or goals.\n\n34\n00:01:49,026 --> 00:01:52,073\nMake small choices, like which problem set comes first,\n\n35\n00:01:52,093 --> 00:01:53,397\nand where you study.\n\n36\n00:01:53,825 --> 00:01:55,239\nTrack your own progress,\n\n37\n00:01:55,259 --> 00:01:56,374\nnot just grades.\n\n38\n00:01:56,633 --> 00:01:59,307\nAnd study with people who make the work feel shared.\n\n39\n00:01:59,477 --> 00:02:00,891\nFind your own reason.\n\n40\n00:02:01,069 --> 00:02:02,163\nThen let it pull you.\n\n"
